❯ Interesting, from this it is clear that you have not deeply thought about the ISA and programming model: the checkerboard architecture is a scalable
  block matrix setup, so we basically have two layers of matmul, the compute tile is doing a 16x16 matmul, and the checkerboard is doing a 16x16 block
  matmul, and we can do a set of block matmuls because we can hold a set of matrix tiles in the L3, that we need to maximize the reuse for. So the
  checkerboard is moving blocks of tiles to schedule a blocked matmul, and the compute tiles are executing 16x16 matmuls that represent the constituent
  matmul building block for the block matmul. Because we are doing a system level schedule, the DMA, BLockMover, and Streamer all need their own
  sequencing and address generation capability otherwise it is impossible to support large matmuls like this: we would not have enough program storage
  at the DMA, BlockMover or Streamer to hold all the commands. So, in your ISA analysis, you did not identify this requirement? Can you RCA why you
  missed this functionality. Block matmul is the key to all of this and we have on our KPU SoC a two level block matmul, and when we have multiple KPU
  SoCs, we'll have a three level block matmul to schedule. Please tell me where you went wrong, and why you didn't identify this requirement and design
  option.


● You're absolutely right, and this is an important failure to analyze. Let me do a proper RCA.

  Root Cause Analysis

  What I Got Wrong

  I treated the KPU as a Von Neumann machine where every operation is an explicit instruction. I proposed:
  - 8.4 million explicit tile operations
  - Enumerating every DMA load, every BM move, every STR feed
  - "Assembling" a program with millions of instructions

  This is fundamentally wrong for three reasons:

  1. Program Storage is Finite

  A DMA engine, BlockMover, or Streamer has a small instruction buffer (maybe 64-256 entries). You cannot store millions of instructions. I should have asked: "How do real DMA controllers handle large transfers?"

  Answer: Descriptors with loops and address generation, not explicit per-transfer instructions.

  2. I Ignored the Loop Instructions Already in the ISA

  // These exist in data_movement_isa.hpp - I listed them but didn't understand WHY
  LOOP_BEGIN,  // Start hardware loop
  LOOP_END,    // End hardware loop

  These exist precisely to enable compact programs. A loop with address generation can express millions of operations in ~10 instructions.

  3. I Missed the Hierarchical Block Matrix Structure

  The KPU has two levels of block matmul:

  Level 2: Checkerboard (16×16 compute tiles)
           Each "element" is a 16×16 tile
           Block matmul: C_block += A_block × B_block

  Level 1: Compute Tile (16×16 PEs)
           Each "element" is a float
           Element matmul: C += A × B

  For a 4096×1024 @ 1024×8192 matmul on T256:
  - The checkerboard processes 256×256 element super-tiles (16 compute tiles × 16 elements each)
  - This is a 16×32 @ 4×32 block matmul at the checkerboard level
  - Each block operation triggers a 16×16 tile matmul at compute level

  Why I Missed This
